Subject: SOP Multi-Target Semantic Compiler Purpose Justification

Description: Claim-support and alternative analysis justifying a bounded revision of SOP's purpose to multi-target semantic programming while withholding implementation and execution authority.

@ [justification_uuid] c5a7c854-6934-4a8f-b40f-c13898592543
@ [source_snapshot_uuid] a90af861-ccb4-433f-964d-eff53d7457ad
@ [exhaustion_uuid] d83536fe-85e4-48ca-8203-1518f8f36188
@ [state] justified_unsigned

& [Claim_1_MultiTargetPurpose] is supported
  + [claim] SOP should be canonically understood as an inspectable semantic programming language with multi-target compilation
  + [support] existing signed SOP identities already define semantic units, contracts, programs, transitions, effects, reflection, graph identity, trust, and neural boundaries; the new source unifies their intended direction
  + [confidence] high_for_purpose_formation
  + [counterargument] the repository lacks a complete compiler and could be overclaiming language maturity
  + [answer] the canonical distinguishes semantic purpose from physical implementation and explicitly withholds implementation authority
  + [invalidation] existing core meanings conflict irreconcilably, role compatibility fails, or future implementation proves the purpose internally inconsistent

& [Claim_2_OneSemanticIR] is supported
  + [claim] one source-mapped SemanticIR should be the semantic center for every target
  + [support] a shared center preserves identity, contracts, source correspondence, effects, diagnostics, and cross-target comparison
  + [confidence] high_at_architecture_grade
  + [counterargument] radically different targets may need different internal forms
  + [answer] backends may use target-local forms after the common ABI, but any meaning delta is a declared projection or loss rather than a hidden source fork
  + [invalidation] a target requirement demonstrates that the common IR cannot represent a necessary source distinction without distortion

& [Claim_3_RoleFirstAdmission] is supported
  + [claim] document and region role closure must precede all executable interpretation
  + [support] the existing corpus contains definitions, data, policies, proofs, narratives, tests, profiles, and mixed documents with instruction-shaped text
  + [confidence] very_high
  + [counterargument] explicit roles add friction to concise SOP
  + [answer] default preservation is safer; profiles may provide concise explicit role declarations without inferring authority
  + [invalidation] none without changing the source-preservation and execution-safety goals

& [Claim_4_ExactNeuralHybrid] is supported
  + [claim] exact, neural, and hybrid targets should be distinct first-class profiles
  + [support] exact semantics are needed for identity, constraints, effects, and replay; neural behavior is useful for routing and articulation; hybrid gating preserves both advantages
  + [confidence] high_for_boundary_low_for_unimplemented_performance
  + [counterargument] neural output may never satisfy useful semantic correspondence
  + [answer] the profile may remain advisory or be rejected; no performance or equivalence benefit is presumed
  + [invalidation] measured trials show no benefit over exact retrieval or cannot satisfy declared safety and correspondence gates

& [Claim_5_LLMAsCandidateFormer] is supported
  + [claim] an LLM can assist compilation by forming candidate structures but cannot certify them
  + [support] models can articulate and normalize large semantic corpora, while deterministic checks can verify syntax, identity, digest, schema, effect, and correspondence constraints
  + [confidence] high_for_assistance_not_for_direct_weight_correctness
  + [counterargument] model-generated artifacts may be subtly inconsistent or fabricated
  + [answer] all model output remains proposed, source-linked, independently checked, and rejectable
  + [invalidation] the candidate pipeline cannot preserve provenance or produces unacceptable defect rates under measured fixtures

& [Claim_6_CommonABIs] is supported
  + [claim] common target and runtime ABIs should separate compilation from admission and execution
  + [support] explicit seams make capability, loss, proofs, authority, effects, and failures testable across backends
  + [confidence] high_at_logical_design_grade
  + [counterargument] an early ABI could freeze premature physical decisions
  + [answer] the ABI is semantic and technology-neutral; exact encodings and APIs remain versioned implementation profiles
  + [invalidation] implementation reveals a missing semantic category requiring a new source-visible ABI revision

& [Claim_7_IndependentBootstrap] is supported
  + [claim] reflective self-description requires an independently trusted bootstrap and verifier
  + [support] a compiler cannot establish its own correctness solely by reusing its own parsing and expected-output path
  + [confidence] very_high
  + [counterargument] complete self-hosting is a normal language milestone
  + [answer] self-hosting can reduce the trusted base progressively but never makes circular evidence sufficient
  + [invalidation] only by adopting a different proof theory explicitly capable of grounding the bootstrap, which itself requires new source

& [Claim_8_BoundedSignature] is supported
  + [claim] the current signature should authorize purpose semantics and future specification routing but no implementation or execution
  + [support] the source is architectural, current worker artifacts define interfaces, and no physical compiler or conformance proof was produced in this slice
  + [confidence] very_high
  + [counterargument] withholding even the minimum fixture delays empirical learning
  + [answer] the fixture is already bounded for the next implementation slice; a separate release makes its evidence honest and reviewable
  + [invalidation] user explicitly changes scope through new preserved source and governance processing

& [AlternativeComparison] favors the selected architecture
  + [AI_only_compiler] rejected because it loses deterministic authority and non-neural projections
  + [exact_only_language] rejected because it excludes the source's learned-fabric purpose
  + [backend_specific_languages] rejected because semantic identity and proof fragment
  + [database_only_engine] rejected because query and storage cannot express complete program, effect, runtime, and conformance semantics alone
  + [neural_authoritative_store] rejected because exact source identity, provenance, revocation, and proof cannot depend on approximate weights
  + [implementation_first] rejected for this slice because it would choose physical details before the purpose and compatibility boundary is authoritative

& [BenefitCostRisk] is proportionate
  + [benefit] one coherent language purpose connects current SOP contracts, Cantor query, faculties, neural routing, workflows, interfaces, and future hardware targets
  + [cost] governance artifacts, future profile work, compiler implementation, conformance infrastructure, and migration discipline
  + [primary_risk] language-completeness overclaim
  + [mitigation] explicit formation-versus-implementation boundary and requirement statuses
  + [secondary_risk] target proliferation
  + [mitigation] signed target-growth gate and measured benefit
  + [security_risk] instruction-shaped data or provider output gains effect authority
  + [mitigation] role-first admission, effect rows, trust checks, and runtime broker

& [Decision] is justified
  + adopt the bounded multi-target semantic programming purpose
  + make exact semantics and source correspondence authoritative
  + admit neural behavior only as declared proposal or narrower proved target behavior
  + preserve historical SOP under explicit roles and no implicit execution
  + require independent conformance and bootstrap
  + issue no implementation, runtime, provider, training, hardware, or effect authority
